{id:4181,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'In Selenium Grid 4, what is the primary role of the Node component in a distributed architecture?',options:['To accept client requests and route them to available sessions','To execute tests by registering available browser slots and managing browser driver processes on host machines','To manage the session queue and store test logs in a database','To serve the GraphQL API endpoint and proxy web traffic'],answer:1,explanation:'In Selenium Grid 4, a Node is responsible for hosting actual browser instances. It registers its available browser capabilities (browser slots) with the Distributor and creates, manages, and terminates browser driver processes upon receiving session commands from the grid.'},
{id:4182,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does Selenium Grid match an incoming `Capabilities` request to an appropriate Node slot?',options:['By matching randomly across any connected Node regardless of capabilities','By comparing requested capabilities like `browserName`, `platformName`, and `browserVersion` against stereotypes registered by active Nodes','By evaluating the IP address of the test runner machine','By executing a JavaScript evaluation script on each node sequentially'],answer:1,explanation:'When a client requests a new RemoteWebDriver session, the Grid Distributor checks the desired capabilities (such as browserName, browserVersion, and platformName) against the stereotype configurations defined by connected Nodes to locate a matching browser slot.'},
{id:4183,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How is a Node configured with specific browser capabilities using a TOML configuration file in Selenium Grid 4?',options:['Inside a [node.docker] section using YAML syntax','Inside the [[node.driver-configuration]] section by specifying display-name, max-sessions, and stereotype capabilities like browserName="chrome"','Inside pom.xml under maven-compiler-plugin','Inside testng.xml under the <grid-config> tag'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Grid 4 uses TOML configuration files where `[[node.driver-configuration]]` blocks define driver execution details, maximum concurrent sessions, and `stereotype` tables specifying exact browserName, browserVersion, and platformName attributes supported by that machine.'},
{id:4184,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What happens in Selenium Grid 4 if a client requests `browserVersion: "120"` but connected nodes only advertise `browserVersion: "119"` and `browserVersion: "121"`?',options:['The Grid automatically downloads and installs version 120 on the Node','The Grid rejects the session request with a SessionNotCreatedException because no Node satisfies the requested stereotype','The Grid launches version 121 assuming backwards compatibility','The Grid runs the test in headless Firefox instead'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Grid enforces strict capability matching for specific version requests. If no connected Node provides a stereotype matching the requested browserVersion, the Distributor rejects the session and returns a SessionNotCreatedException.'},
{id:4185,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the default URL and port for accessing the Selenium Grid 4 web management dashboard?',options:['http://localhost:8080/ui','http://localhost:4444/ui','http://localhost:5555/dashboard','http://localhost:9222/grid'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Grid 4 serves its built-in web management console at `http://localhost:4444/ui` (or `http://<grid-host>:4444/ui`), displaying active sessions, queued requests, registered nodes, and browser slot allocations in real-time with an interactive graphical interface.'},
{id:4186,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which endpoint in Selenium Grid 4 provides GraphQL API access for querying Grid status, active sessions, and node health?',options:['GET http://localhost:4444/status/json','POST http://localhost:4444/graphql','GET http://localhost:4444/api/v1/health','POST http://localhost:4444/wd/hub/graphql'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Grid 4 provides a GraphQL query endpoint at `/graphql` on port 4444. Automation engineers and monitoring tools can execute GraphQL queries over HTTP POST to inspect nodes, session queues, and system utilization metrics.'},
{id:4187,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Which GraphQL query field can be used to inspect queued session requests waiting for available slots in Selenium Grid 4?',options:['grid.nodeRegistry.failedSessions','grid.sessionQueueRequests or sessionQueueSize','distributor.pendingDrivers','node.idleSlots'],answer:1,explanation:'In Selenium Grid 4 GraphQL schema, the `grid` and `sessionQueue` queries expose fields like `sessionQueueRequests` and `sessionQueueSize`, allowing monitoring dashboards to observe real-time queue depth and wait times for overloaded grids.'},
{id:4188,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is Selenium Manager, introduced in Selenium 4.6+?',options:['A paid cloud testing platform hosted by Software Freedom Conservancy','An automated binary management tool bundled directly into Selenium core to automatically download and configure matching browser drivers','A GUI desktop application for recording and editing Selenium scripts','A Jenkins plugin for orchestrating parallel Maven test suites'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Manager is a built-in CLI tool (written in Rust) bundled into Selenium 4.6+. It automatically detects installed browser versions (Chrome, Firefox, Edge) and downloads matching driver binaries (chromedriver, geckodriver, msedgedriver) without requiring manual setup or third-party libraries.'},
{id:4189,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does Selenium Manager alter typical WebDriver initialization code compared to older Selenium 3 approaches?',options:['You must explicitly call SeleniumManager.downloadDriver() in every test','You no longer need System.setProperty("webdriver.chrome.driver", "...") or WebDriverManager.chromedriver().setup() before initializing new ChromeDriver()','You must pass a valid API license key into driver options','You are required to run tests inside a Docker container'],answer:1,explanation:'Because Selenium Manager is invoked automatically under the hood by Selenium 4.6+ during driver initialization, developers can directly write `WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();` without configuring system path properties or importing external WebDriverManager libraries.'},
{id:4190,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can you configure custom proxy or cache directories for Selenium Manager when running behind an enterprise corporate firewall?',options:['By modifying the browser user agent header in ChromeOptions','Via environment variables such as `SE_PROXY` and `SE_CACHE_PATH` or Selenium configuration files (se.toml)','By hardcoding proxy settings inside the JDK security policy file','Selenium Manager does not support corporate proxy configurations'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Manager respects standard environment variables like `SE_PROXY` (or `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY`) and `SE_CACHE_PATH` to direct driver binary downloads through corporate proxy gateways and store cached binaries in specified local storage directories.'},
{id:4191,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Selenoid, developed by Aerokube, in the context of Selenium test execution?',options:['A Java assertion library for Selenium elements','A lightweight Docker-based implementation of the Selenium Grid hub written in Go that runs each browser session in an isolated container','A record-and-playback browser extension for Chrome','An AI-powered locator generator for flaky elements'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenoid is an open-source, lightweight alternative to standard Selenium Grid written in Go. It dynamically spins up a clean, isolated Docker container for each requested browser session and destroys it immediately after the session ends.'},
{id:4192,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which custom capability is commonly passed to Selenoid to enable live VNC viewing and video recording of test execution?',options:['selenoid:options -> {"enableVNC": true, "enableVideo": true}','chrome:recording -> {"vnc": "active", "record": 1}','docker:video -> "record_all"','grid:stream -> {"vnc": true}'],answer:0,explanation:'Selenoid supports a specialized `selenoid:options` map inside desired capabilities where flags like `enableVNC: true` (for live interaction/viewing via Selenoid UI) and `enableVideo: true` (for recording MP4 screen video) can be specified.'},
{id:4193,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What was the primary innovation of Zalenium when it was widely used for Selenium Grid infrastructure?',options:['It provided an in-memory SQL database for test logs','It provided a dynamic Docker-based Selenium Grid with built-in live preview, video recording of tests, and auto-scaling containers on demand','It replaced W3C WebDriver protocol with raw HTTP requests','It eliminated the need for browser drivers entirely'],answer:1,explanation:'Zalenium was a popular Docker-based Selenium Grid solution that automatically scaled browser nodes in containers based on test demand and recorded video for every test session alongside an interactive live web dashboard.'},
{id:4194,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the Page Component pattern in Selenium test automation architecture?',options:['Writing all test assertions inside a single giant Page Object class','Decomposing complex web pages into small, reusable component classes (such as NavigationBar, Header, Pagination, Modal) embedded within Page Objects','A method for injecting React components into the DOM at runtime','A design pattern where each HTML tag has its own Java class'],answer:1,explanation:'The Page Component pattern breaks down reusable UI widgets (like header menus, search bars, modals, and tables) into dedicated component classes. Page objects then compose these component instances rather than duplicating locator and interaction logic across multiple pages.'},
{id:4195,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does a Page Component class typically scope its element locators to avoid selecting elements outside its container?',options:['By searching only inside the global driver with absolute XPath','By accepting a root `WebElement` container (or parent By locator) in its constructor and calling `container.findElement(By...)` for child elements','By clearing the browser DOM before each component interaction','By using JavaScript alerts to isolate component boundaries'],answer:1,explanation:'A component class typically receives a root `WebElement` (or locator) representing the component container. Internal queries call `rootElement.findElement(By...)` to find descendants strictly within that container, preventing accidental selection of identically styled elements elsewhere on the page.'},
{id:4196,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Fluent Page Object pattern (method chaining) in Selenium test automation?',options:['Writing test scripts in JavaScript rather than Java','Designing page object methods to return `this` or the next target `PageObject` instance, enabling fluent method chaining in test methods','Using CSS animations to make page transitions smoother','Executing test steps asynchronously using CompletableFuture'],answer:1,explanation:'The Fluent Page Object pattern designs action methods to return a Page Object instance representing the resulting page state (or `this` for intra-page actions). This enables readable, chained test flows like `loginPage.enterUser("admin").enterPass("123").clickSubmit().verifyDashboardVisible();`.'},
{id:4197,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a Fluent Page Object flow, what should a method like `clickSubmitExpectingFailure()` return compared to `clickSubmitSuccess()`?',options:['Both methods must return void','`clickSubmitExpectingFailure()` should return the current `LoginPage` (or `this`), while `clickSubmitSuccess()` should return the resulting `DashboardPage`','Both methods must return a boolean primitive','`clickSubmitSuccess()` should return null'],answer:1,explanation:'Fluent page objects reflect user journey state transitions. When an action stays on the same page due to validation failure, returning `this` (or `LoginPage`) allows further error checks, whereas a successful login returns a new `DashboardPage` instance.'},
{id:4198,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can Selenium 4 Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) be used to inspect or monitor WebSocket communication in a web application?',options:['By calling driver.manage().logs().get("websocket")','By enabling the CDP `Network` domain and listening to `Network.webSocketCreated`, `Network.webSocketFrameSent`, and `Network.webSocketFrameReceived` events','By injecting a custom WebSocket mock via Robot class','WebSocket connections cannot be observed using CDP'],answer:1,explanation:'Using Selenium 4 DevTools (`driver.getDevTools()`), you can create an active CDP session, execute `Network.enable()`, and register listeners to CDP events such as `Network.webSocketCreated()`, `Network.webSocketFrameSent()`, and `Network.webSocketFrameReceived()` to capture bidirectional WebSocket frames.'},
{id:4199,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you handle basic HTTP Authentication (browser auth popups) in modern Selenium 4 without third-party tools?',options:['Using driver.switchTo().alert().sendKeys("user:pass")','Using `((HasAuthentication) driver).register(UsernameAndPassword.of("admin", "secret"))` via CDP/BiDi credentials registration','By clicking the popup window with Java AWT Robot','HTTP authentication is completely unsupported in W3C WebDriver'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4 introduces the `HasAuthentication` interface. Invoking `((HasAuthentication) driver).register(UsernameAndPassword.of("admin", "secret"))` intercepts HTTP 401 basic/digest challenges and supplies valid credentials directly over CDP/BiDi network domains without requiring native OS dialog interaction.'},
{id:4200,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the traditional URL-embedded credentials approach for bypassing basic HTTP authentication in Selenium?',options:['driver.get("http://username:password@example.com/protected")','driver.navigate().to("http://example.com?auth=username:password")','driver.get("http://example.com/#username#password")','driver.manage().addCookie("auth", "username:password")'],answer:0,explanation:'Embedding credentials directly in the URL formatted as `http://username:password@example.com` has historically provided a way to bypass basic authentication dialogs, although modern browsers increasingly restrict or deprecate this format in favor of CDP authentication.'},
{id:4201,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why can standard `driver.findElement()` calls fail intermittently when testing Single Page Applications (SPAs) built with React or Vue?',options:['React and Vue disable standard DOM events permanently','SPA frameworks render and update DOM nodes dynamically via asynchronous virtual DOM reconciliation, causing elements to not exist immediately upon navigation','SPAs run only in WebGL canvas mode','Selenium cannot parse HTML5 web components'],answer:1,explanation:'SPAs built with React or Vue update the DOM asynchronously through JavaScript execution and virtual DOM patching. Finding elements immediately after an action without explicit waiting often results in NoSuchElementException or StaleElementReferenceException.'},
{id:4202,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "client-side hydration" in modern SSR/SPA frameworks (React, Angular, Vue), and how does it affect Selenium automation?',options:['A mechanism for cleaning up memory leaks in browser tabs','The phase where client-side JavaScript attaches event listeners and state to server-rendered HTML; clicking elements before hydration completes can result in unresponsive clicks','The process of downloading browser extensions in headless mode','An automated cache-clearing technique in modern web servers'],answer:1,explanation:'Hydration occurs when client-side JavaScript boots up and attaches event listeners to pre-rendered HTML sent by the server. If Selenium clicks a button before hydration finishes, the click registers visually on the DOM but triggers no JavaScript handler.'},
{id:4203,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can a custom wait condition verify that React or Vue rendering / hydration is complete before interacting with an element?',options:['By executing Thread.sleep(10000) before every step','By waiting with JavascriptExecutor until a custom window flag (e.g., `window.__APP_READY__`) or framework state indicator is true, or waiting for specific interactive DOM states','By clearing the browser local storage on every click','By restarting the browser driver on each route change'],answer:1,explanation:'Robust SPA automation uses explicit waits checking application-level readiness signals, such as checking `window.performance.timing`, React/Vue root mounting flags (e.g. `document.querySelector("#root")._reactRootContainer`), or waiting for specific UI data attributes that signal complete hydration and component readiness.'},
{id:4204,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Why is `data-testid` (or `data-test`, `data-cy`) considered an industry best practice for Selenium element locators?',options:['Because it makes the browser render the webpage 50% faster','Because dedicated test attributes are decoupled from styling (CSS classes) and structural hierarchy (XPath), preventing test fragility during UI redesigns','Because browsers reject tests that use class or ID locators','Because data-testid attributes automatically bypass explicit waits'],answer:1,explanation:'Dedicated test attributes like `data-testid` decouple test selectors from fragile CSS class names and volatile DOM hierarchies. Developers can freely refactor styling, layout, and HTML tags without unintentionally breaking automated end-to-end Selenium test suites.'},
{id:4205,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How should accessible `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` attributes be used in Selenium locators?',options:['Only when testing screen reader software on mobile devices','As robust, semantically meaningful selectors (e.g., `By.cssSelector("[aria-label=\'Close dialog\']")`) that simultaneously promote web accessibility compliance','They should never be used because accessibility attributes change frequently','Only via JavaScript executor calls'],answer:1,explanation:'Locating elements via `aria-label` or role attributes (e.g., `By.cssSelector("[aria-label=\'Submit Order\']")`) aligns test locators with accessible user experiences. Writing tests against accessibility attributes incentivizes development teams to maintain high accessibility standards.'},
{id:4206,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why is using extensive `//` (descendant-or-self) queries in deep DOM trees considered an XPath performance anti-pattern?',options:['Because the XPath engine must recursively traverse and evaluate every single descendant node from the root, causing noticeable latency in large documents','Because `//` is deprecated in XPath 2.0','Because `//` cannot find elements that have text nodes','Because browsers throw a SyntaxError when evaluating `//`'],answer:0,explanation:'Using `//tag` instructs the XPath engine to recursively search all descendant branches from the context node. In applications with large, deep DOM trees, multiple unconstrained `//` queries cause significant evaluation overhead compared to direct child `/` paths or targeted CSS selectors.'},
{id:4207,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which XPath expression is more performant and targeted for locating a submit button inside a specific form?',options:['//form//div//div//span//button[@type="submit"]','//form[@id="login-form"]/descendant-or-self::node()','//form[@id="login-form"]//button[@type="submit"]','/descendant::button'],answer:2,explanation:'Locating the container element by its unique ID first and narrowing down the search to `//button[@type="submit"]` provides an optimal balance of locator maintainability and XPath engine search efficiency compared to over-specified chains or unbounded wildcards.'},
{id:4208,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In CSS selectors for Selenium, what does the prefix match operator `^=` do (e.g., `input[name^="user_"]`)?',options:['Matches elements whose attribute value ends with "user_"','Matches elements whose attribute value contains the substring "user_"','Matches elements whose attribute value starts with the prefix "user_"','Matches elements that do not contain "user_"'],answer:2,explanation:'In CSS attribute selectors, `^=` denotes a starts-with match. For instance, `input[name^="user_"]` matches `<input name="user_email">` and `<input name="user_name">`, which is particularly effective for matching dynamic IDs that share a common prefix across rendered components.'},
{id:4209,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In CSS selectors for Selenium, what does the suffix match operator `$=` do (e.g., `button[id$="_submit"]`)?',options:['Matches elements whose attribute value ends with "_submit"','Matches elements whose attribute value starts with "_submit"','Matches elements whose price is in dollars','Matches elements containing "_submit" anywhere in any attribute'],answer:0,explanation:'In CSS attribute selectors, `$=` denotes an ends-with match. For example, `button[id$="_submit"]` matches `<button id="btn_login_submit">` and `<button id="checkout_submit">`, allowing tests to match static suffix tokens while ignoring auto-generated random dynamic prefixes.'},
{id:4210,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In CSS selectors for Selenium, what does the substring match operator `*=` do (e.g., `a[href*="checkout"]`)?',options:['Matches elements where the attribute is an arithmetic product','Matches elements whose attribute value contains the substring "checkout" anywhere within the string','Matches only elements with an exact match for "checkout"','Matches all elements on the page'],answer:1,explanation:'The `*=` operator in CSS matches any element whose target attribute contains the specified substring anywhere within its value. This is widely used in Selenium to target elements with dynamic session IDs or partially generated URLs.'},
{id:4211,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the recommended pattern in Selenium for automating an OAuth login popup window (e.g., "Sign in with Google")?',options:['Record and hardcode the window handle string in the test code','Store the parent window handle with `getWindowHandle()`, click the OAuth button, wait for `getWindowHandles().size() == 2`, switch to the popup handle, complete login, and switch back to parent','Use driver.close() immediately after clicking the OAuth button','Execute a JavaScript alert to copy the session token'],answer:1,explanation:'The standard multi-window pattern involves saving `String parent = driver.getWindowHandle()`, waiting until `driver.getWindowHandles().size() > 1`, iterating handles to find and switch to the popup, completing authentication, and calling `driver.switchTo().window(parent)` after the popup closes.'},
{id:4212,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When testing third-party payment gateways that open in a new window or iframe, what is a key synchronization challenge?',options:['Payment gateways disable HTTP POST requests in test environments','Third-party gateways introduce unpredictable domain redirects, cross-origin security boundaries, and varying load times requiring explicit waits for URL changes or frame readiness','WebDriver cannot switch between different domains in the same session','Cookies cannot be stored across different browser tabs'],answer:1,explanation:'Payment gateways involve cross-origin redirects, varying network latency, and dynamic iframe/window rendering. Tests must use explicit waits for expected domain URLs, iframe availability, and elements rather than static sleeps to avoid race conditions.'},
{id:4213,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'In Selenium 4, how do you open a new browser tab and automatically switch focus to it in one command?',options:['driver.navigate().newTab()','driver.switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.TAB)','driver.findElement(By.tagName("body")).sendKeys(Keys.CONTROL + "t")','driver.manage().window().openTab()'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4 introduced the `driver.switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.TAB)` API, which creates a brand new browser tab and immediately switches WebDriver focus to it in a single clean W3C-compliant command without requiring manual handle enumeration.'},
{id:4214,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'In Selenium 4, how do you open a completely separate OS-level browser window and switch focus to it?',options:['driver.switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.WINDOW)','driver.openNewBrowser()','driver.manage().window().maximize()','driver.get("chrome://new-window")'],answer:0,explanation:'Calling `driver.switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.WINDOW)` creates an independent top-level browser window (separate from existing tabs) and automatically switches the active WebDriver context to it in a single clean W3C-compliant command without requiring extra window handle logic.'},
{id:4215,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of `driver.close()` versus `driver.quit()` when working with multiple browser tabs in Selenium?',options:['`driver.close()` closes all tabs and terminates the driver process; `driver.quit()` only closes the active tab','`driver.close()` closes only the currently focused browser tab/window; `driver.quit()` closes all windows/tabs and safely terminates the entire WebDriver session process','`driver.close()` clears cookies; `driver.quit()` clears cache','Both methods behave identically in Selenium 4'],answer:1,explanation:'`driver.close()` closes the current window or tab that WebDriver is focused on. If other windows remain open, the session stays active. `driver.quit()` shuts down all browser windows, stops the driver server process, and releases system resources.'},
{id:4216,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Selenide, and what core problems does it solve compared to raw Selenium WebDriver in Java?',options:['It is a JavaScript compiler for Selenium code','It is a wrapper around Selenium WebDriver that provides concise syntax, automatic smart waiting for elements, transparent AJAX handling, and automatic browser lifecycle management','It is a cloud testing provider like SauceLabs','It is an alternative to JUnit for running test assertions'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenide is a high-level test framework built on top of Selenium WebDriver. It eliminates boilerplate code by providing concise methods (like `$("#submit").click()`), automatic smart waiting for elements and AJAX calls, and automated browser initialization and teardown.'},
{id:4217,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In Selenide, how do you find an element by CSS selector and assert its text and visibility in a single readable line?',options:['driver.findElement(By.css("#message")).getText().equals("Success");','$("#message").shouldBe(visible).shouldHave(text("Success"));','Assert.assertTrue(Selenide.find("#message").isDisplayed());','driver.verify("#message", "Success");'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenide provides fluent methods like `$("#message").shouldBe(visible).shouldHave(text("Success"))`. It automatically polls the DOM until the condition is satisfied or a configurable timeout expires, completely eliminating fragile explicit `WebDriverWait` boilerplate code throughout your tests.'},
{id:4218,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does Selenide handle `StaleElementReferenceException` during test execution?',options:['It immediately crashes the test suite to alert the developer','It automatically catches StaleElementReferenceException, re-evaluates the locator against the refreshed DOM, and retries the action within its timeout window','It reloads the whole browser page via driver.navigate().refresh()','It converts the element into a static bitmap image'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenide implements lazy element proxies. When an interaction encounters a `StaleElementReferenceException`, Selenide catches the exception, re-queries the DOM using the original locator, and retries the interaction until the timeout expires.'},
{id:4219,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you load a custom browser extension (.crx file) into a Chrome session in Selenium Java?',options:['By copying the extension file to the system desktop','By calling `ChromeOptions.addExtensions(new File("path/to/extension.crx"))` before instantiating ChromeDriver','By executing `driver.get("chrome://extensions/install")`','Extensions cannot be loaded into automated Chrome instances'],answer:1,explanation:'`ChromeOptions` provides the `addExtensions(File... extensionFiles)` method, which packs and loads specified `.crx` extension binary files directly into the Chrome browser profile upon session startup, enabling full automated testing of extension functionality.'},
{id:4220,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you load an unpacked Chrome browser extension from a directory in Selenium?',options:['options.addArguments("--load-extension=/path/to/extension/folder");','options.setDirectory("/path/to/extension");','driver.installExtension("/path/to/extension");','driver.manage().extensions().addFolder("/path/to/folder");'],answer:0,explanation:'Using `options.addArguments("--load-extension=/path/to/extension/folder")` instructs the Chromium browser process to load an unpacked development extension directory directly from the local file system upon launching the automated browser instance during test session initialization.'},
{id:4221,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why do standard Selenium `getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE)` calls on Chrome only capture the visible viewport rather than the full scrollable web page?',options:['Because of a bug in Java BufferedImage library','Because standard W3C WebDriver screenshot specification defines capturing only the current visible viewport area by default','Because full-page screenshots are restricted by HTML5 security policies','Because browser drivers run out of RAM when capturing full pages'],answer:1,explanation:'The W3C WebDriver specification explicitly defines `Take Screenshot` as capturing the top-level browsing context\'s visible viewport. Consequently, content located below the fold requires manual scrolling or specialized CDP/library capabilities to capture fully.'},
{id:4222,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does the AShot library capture a full-page screenshot in Selenium WebDriver?',options:['By resizing the computer physical monitor resolution','By scrolling the viewport incrementally, capturing screenshots at each step, and stitching the image tiles together into a single BufferedImage','By converting HTML into a PDF file','By intercepting network image requests via CDP'],answer:1,explanation:'AShot uses a scrolling shooting strategy (`ShootingStrategies.viewportPasting(100)`). It scrolls down the page in increments matching the viewport height, captures screenshots of each segment, and stitches them seamlessly into one composite full-page image.'},
{id:4223,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'In Selenium 4 (Chromium-based browsers), how can you capture a full-page screenshot natively using Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP)?',options:['By calling driver.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FULL_PAGE)','By sending the CDP command `Page.captureScreenshot` with parameter `captureBeyondViewport: true` and metrics from `Page.getLayoutMetrics`','By calling driver.executeScript("window.print()")','By pressing Ctrl+Shift+P via Actions class'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4 CDP support enables calling `Page.captureScreenshot` with `captureBeyondViewport: true` (and matching viewport dimensions from `Page.getLayoutMetrics`). This instructs Chrome to render and capture the full scrollable height directly without manual stitching.'},
{id:4224,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the primary benefit of using Hamcrest matchers with Selenium assertions?',options:['Hamcrest executes tests in parallel automatically','Hamcrest provides readable, expressive matchers (e.g., `assertThat(title, containsString("Login"))`) and detailed failure messages explaining expected vs actual state','Hamcrest replaces the need for browser drivers','Hamcrest automatically fixes broken locators'],answer:1,explanation:'Hamcrest improves test assertion readability and diagnostics. When an assertion fails, Hamcrest produces clear error messages detailing what was expected versus what was actually found (e.g., `Expected: a string containing "Login" but was "Error 404"`).'},
{id:4225,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you write a fluent assertion using AssertJ to verify multiple properties of a list of WebElements in Selenium?',options:['Assert.assertEquals(elements.size(), 3); elements.forEach(...)','assertThat(elements).extracting(WebElement::getText).hasSize(3).containsExactly("Home", "Products", "Contact");','AssertJ.checkAll(elements, "Home", "Products", "Contact");','driver.assertList(elements, 3);'],answer:1,explanation:'AssertJ enables fluent assertions on collections. Using `assertThat(elements).extracting(WebElement::getText).hasSize(3).containsExactly("Home", "Products", "Contact")` extracts the text of each element and validates the entire collection size, text contents, and exact ordering in a single expressive statement.'},
{id:4226,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the signature of the `apply` method when implementing a custom `ExpectedCondition<T>` in Selenium Java?',options:['public void apply(WebDriver driver)','public T apply(WebDriver driver)','public boolean execute(WebElement element)','public String verify(TakesScreenshot driver)'],answer:1,explanation:'`ExpectedCondition<T>` is a functional interface in `org.openqa.selenium.support.ui` extending `Function<WebDriver, T>`. Its single abstract method is `T apply(WebDriver input)`, which returns an object of type `T` (or `null`/`false` to signal wait continuation).'},
{id:4227,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How should a custom `ExpectedCondition<Boolean>` handle `NoSuchElementException` or `StaleElementReferenceException` inside its `apply` method?',options:['Let the exceptions crash the JVM immediately','Catch the exceptions and return null or false, allowing FluentWait to continue polling until the timeout expires','Rethrow them wrapped in a RuntimeException','Call driver.quit() inside the catch block'],answer:1,explanation:'In a custom ExpectedCondition, catching transient exceptions like `NoSuchElementException` and returning `false` or `null` informs `FluentWait` that the condition is not yet satisfied, prompting it to sleep for the polling interval and retry before timing out.'},
{id:4228,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you implement a test retry mechanism in TestNG for flaky Selenium test methods?',options:['By annotating test classes with @Test(retry = 3)','By creating a class implementing `IRetryAnalyzer`, overriding `retry(ITestResult result)`, and attaching it via `@Test(retryAnalyzer = Retry.class)` or an `IAnnotationTransformer` listener','By calling driver.navigate().refresh() in a loop','By wrapping test methods in a while(true) block'],answer:1,explanation:'In TestNG, you implement the `IRetryAnalyzer` interface to define retry logic (counting attempts against a maximum limit). You attach it to test methods via `@Test(retryAnalyzer = Retry.class)` or apply it globally to all tests using an `IAnnotationTransformer`.'},
{id:4229,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In JUnit 5, what annotation or extension can be used to retry a failed Selenium test method a specific number of times?',options:['@RepeatedTest or third-party extensions like `@RetryingTest` (from rerunner-jupiter)','@Test(retryCount = 3)','@TestNGRetry','@Rule RetryRule'],answer:0,explanation:'While JUnit 5 natively provides `@RepeatedTest` for repeated runs regardless of outcome, retrying specifically on failure is accomplished via JUnit 5 extensions like `rerunner-jupiter` (`@RetryingTest(maxAttempts = 3)`) or custom test execution exception handler extensions.'},
{id:4230,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the primary root cause of test flakiness in Selenium web automation?',options:['Using Java instead of Python','Asynchronous UI updates, animation delays, and race conditions caused by inadequate synchronization (e.g., missing explicit waits or relying on static Thread.sleep)','Having more than 5 tests in a test class','Running tests on Linux operating systems'],answer:1,explanation:'The predominant cause of flakiness is timing and synchronization issues. When tests interact with DOM elements before animations complete, AJAX calls finish, or hydration ends without proper explicit waits, tests fail intermittently.'},
{id:4231,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why is relying on `Thread.sleep()` in Selenium tests considered a major contributor to flaky and slow test suites?',options:['`Thread.sleep()` crashes the browser engine','It pauses test execution for a fixed arbitrary duration regardless of whether the element is ready, making tests unnecessarily slow when elements load fast and flaky when networks lag','`Thread.sleep()` is deprecated in modern Java versions','It disables browser JavaScript execution during the sleep'],answer:1,explanation:'`Thread.sleep()` halts execution unconditionally. If an element takes 100ms to appear, a 5-second sleep wastes 4.9 seconds; if network congestion delays loading to 5.1 seconds, the test fails anyway. Explicit dynamic waits poll until ready.'},
{id:4232,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What strategy should be used to optimize Selenium test suite execution speed in CI/CD pipelines?',options:['Add Thread.sleep(5000) after every step to ensure stability','Run tests in parallel across multiple worker threads/nodes, execute headless browsers, reuse browser state via API login/cookies, and eliminate redundant UI navigation','Run tests one by one on a single VM during peak hours','Disable assertions in regression test suites'],answer:1,explanation:'Test suite speed is maximized by parallelizing runs across containers/Grid nodes, using headless browser execution, setting up test state/authentication directly via REST APIs or cookies, and avoiding static sleep calls throughout the codebase.'},
{id:4233,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can bypassing the UI login flow using API requests or direct cookie injection accelerate a Selenium test suite?',options:['It disables the application security firewall','It avoids repeating the slow, browser-rendered multi-step login form before every single test scenario by generating an auth token via API and injecting it directly into browser cookies/localStorage','It prevents the browser from loading CSS stylesheets','It eliminates the need to initialize WebDriver'],answer:1,explanation:'Logging in through the UI before every test adds massive overhead. Making a fast HTTP POST request to obtain an authentication session token and injecting it into browser cookies allows tests to start directly on target pages.'},
{id:4234,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Gauge test automation framework, and how does it integrate with Selenium?',options:['A browser driver specifically built for mobile Safari','An open-source BDD-style test automation framework created by ThoughtWorks that uses Markdown syntax for specifications and executes underlying Selenium automation steps in Java, Python, or C#','A replacement for ChromeDriver built into Google Chrome','A tool for load testing web servers with virtual users'],answer:1,explanation:'Gauge is a lightweight test automation framework by ThoughtWorks. It allows teams to author test specifications in readable Markdown format and implements step implementations in standard programming languages using Selenium WebDriver.'},
{id:4235,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'In Robot Framework, what is the standard library used to control web browsers via Selenium?',options:['BrowserControlLib','SeleniumLibrary','WebAutomationKit','PuppeteerLibrary'],answer:1,explanation:'`SeleniumLibrary` is the official and standard web testing library for Robot Framework. It wraps Selenium WebDriver internally, providing high-level keyword-driven actions such as `Open Browser`, `Input Text`, and `Click Button` for readable automated test suites.'},
{id:4236,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a Robot Framework test suite, what keyword is typically used to open a browser and navigate to a URL using SeleniumLibrary?',options:['Launch App    ${URL}','Open Browser    https://example.com    chrome','Start Chrome    ${URL}','driver.get(https://example.com)'],answer:1,explanation:'In Robot Framework with SeleniumLibrary, the `Open Browser` keyword takes the target URL and desired browser name as arguments: `Open Browser    https://example.com    chrome`, initiating the WebDriver session and navigating to the specified page.'},
{id:4237,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does Jenkins capture and display Selenium test results when tests are run via Maven Surefire or TestNG?',options:['By reading the raw console log output only','By using the "Publish JUnit test result report" post-build action pointing to `**/target/surefire-reports/*.xml` or `**/target/failsafe-reports/*.xml`','By opening a browser on the Jenkins master node','By taking screenshots of the Jenkins workspace directory'],answer:1,explanation:'Maven Surefire and Failsafe plugins generate standardized JUnit XML report files in the `target/*-reports` directories. Jenkins uses the JUnit Plugin to parse these XML files, creating interactive trend graphs, test counts, and failure traces.'},
{id:4238,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In the Maven build lifecycle, what is the difference between the `test` phase and the `integration-test` (verify) phase for running Selenium tests?',options:['The `test` phase runs unit tests via Maven Surefire plugin; the `integration-test` and `verify` phases run end-to-end Selenium tests via Maven Failsafe plugin, ensuring post-integration teardown occurs','The `test` phase only compiles code without running anything','The `verify` phase is only executed when running on Windows','Both phases execute the exact same plugin with identical lifecycle bindings'],answer:0,explanation:'Maven Surefire executes during the `test` phase for unit testing. For Selenium integration tests, Maven Failsafe is bound to `integration-test` and `verify` phases so that environment teardown runs even if test assertions fail during execution.'},
{id:4239,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of the `<argLine>` configuration in `maven-surefire-plugin` when executing Selenium tests?',options:['It specifies the command-line arguments passed to the browser binary','It passes JVM arguments, memory limits (e.g. `-Xmx2048m`), Java agents (such as AspectJ for Allure reports), and system properties to the forked test JVM process','It defines the list of test classes to be compiled','It configures the proxy settings for Git clone operations'],answer:1,explanation:'The `<argLine>` parameter in Maven Surefire/Failsafe passes JVM parameters (like heap size `-Xmx2048m`, encoding `-Dfile.encoding=UTF-8`, or `-javaagent:...` for AspectJ weaver used by Allure) to the forked Java processes running the tests.'},
{id:4240,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When migrating a test framework from Selenium 3 to Selenium 4, what happened to `DesiredCapabilities` for local browser drivers?',options:['DesiredCapabilities is completely required in all Selenium 4 constructors','DesiredCapabilities was replaced by specific browser Options classes (e.g., `ChromeOptions`, `FirefoxOptions`, `EdgeOptions`) matching the W3C WebDriver specification','DesiredCapabilities was renamed to BrowserCapabilities','No changes occurred between Selenium 3 and 4'],answer:1,explanation:'In Selenium 4, `DesiredCapabilities` was deprecated in favor of type-safe, browser-specific Options classes (`ChromeOptions`, `FirefoxOptions`, `SafariOptions`, `EdgeOptions`) conforming strictly to the W3C WebDriver recommendation and eliminating loose untyped key-value capability mappings.'},
{id:4241,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When migrating from Selenium 3 to Selenium 4, how did timeout configuration methods change on `driver.manage().timeouts()`?',options:['Timeouts can no longer be modified in Selenium 4','Methods taking separate numeric time and TimeUnit units were replaced with methods taking `java.time.Duration` (e.g., `implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10))`)','Timeouts are now configured via JSON files only','Timeouts must be passed directly into ChromeDriver constructor'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4 modernized time-based APIs across the framework. Methods like `driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)` were replaced by `driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10))`, standardizing on type-safe Java 8 `java.time.Duration` instances throughout all timeout configuration and polling APIs across the entire library.'},
{id:4242,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What happened to `Actions.pause(long timeout)` and `Actions.moveToElement()` interactions in Selenium 4 migration?',options:['Actions class was completely removed from Selenium 4','`Actions.pause()` now accepts `java.time.Duration` and actions are strictly executed in compliance with W3C Input Specifications','Actions class was moved to an external third-party JAR','Mouse movements were deprecated in favor of touch only'],answer:1,explanation:'In Selenium 4, the `Actions` class was updated to fully comply with W3C WebDriver Action specification. `Actions.pause()` now accepts `Duration`, and complex composite input gestures are encoded as standard W3C input sources.'},
{id:4243,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'In Selenium Grid 4 architecture, what are the six core microservices that can be deployed independently in Distributed mode?',options:['Compiler, Linker, Runner, Reporter, Profiler, Debugger','Event Bus, Session Map, Session Queue, Distributor, Router, and Node','Master, Worker, Watchdog, Proxy, Store, and Logger','Gateway, LoadBalancer, Ingress, Registry, Storage, and Driver'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Grid 4 Distributed mode separates the grid into six distinct microservices: Event Bus (using Netty/Kafka), Session Map, Session Queue, Distributor, Router, and Node, allowing each to be scaled independently in Kubernetes.'},
{id:4244,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the role of the `Router` component in a distributed Selenium Grid 4 cluster?',options:['To compile test scripts into bytecode','To serve as the public front-facing entry point, directing incoming client requests to either the Distributor/SessionQueue (new sessions) or active Nodes (existing sessions) via Session Map','To store screenshots taken during failed test runs','To execute browser automation commands inside containers'],answer:1,explanation:'The Router serves as the unified entry point for Selenium Grid 4. It routes new session requests to the Session Queue and Distributor, while routing ongoing command requests for existing session IDs directly to the appropriate Node looked up in Session Map.'},
{id:4245,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you check the health status of a standalone Selenium Grid 4 instance via CLI or curl?',options:['curl http://localhost:4444/status','curl http://localhost:4444/ping','selenium-cli health --port 4444','curl http://localhost:4444/admin/health'],answer:0,explanation:'Sending an HTTP GET request to `http://localhost:4444/status` returns a structured JSON payload containing the grid\'s readiness status (`"ready": true/false`), registered nodes, available slots, and active sessions for external health checking and monitoring tools.'},
{id:4246,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Where does Selenium Manager store downloaded browser driver binaries on a machine by default?',options:['Inside the Windows System32 directory','In the user home directory under `~/.cache/selenium`','Directly inside the root C:\\ drive','Inside the project\'s `/target` or `/bin` directory'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium Manager maintains a local cache in the user directory (e.g., `~/.cache/selenium` on Linux/macOS or `%USERPROFILE%\.cache\selenium` on Windows) to reuse previously downloaded driver binaries across test executions without repeatedly re-downloading them.'},
{id:4247,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you force Selenium Manager to download automated testing versions of Google Chrome (Chrome for Testing) if Chrome is not installed on the system?',options:['Selenium Manager cannot manage browser binaries, only drivers','Selenium Manager automatically downloads the matching "Chrome for Testing" (CfT) browser binary alongside ChromeDriver if no compatible browser is found','By writing a bash script to install Google Chrome via apt-get','By passing `--download-chrome` to the JVM compiler'],answer:1,explanation:'Starting in Selenium 4.11+, Selenium Manager can manage both browser drivers and browser binaries. If a matching Chrome version is not found locally, Selenium Manager downloads the specific "Chrome for Testing" (CfT) build automatically.'},
{id:4248,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the main configuration file for configuring browsers and video recording in Selenoid?',options:['browsers.json','grid.xml','selenoid.toml','docker-compose.yaml only'],answer:0,explanation:'Selenoid uses a JSON configuration file, typically named `browsers.json`, to define available browser versions, Docker image names (such as `selenoid/vnc:chrome_120.0`), display resolutions, environment variables, default screen geometry, and custom container capabilities.'},
{id:4249,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does Selenoid handle browser container lifecycle per test session?',options:['It runs all tests sequentially inside one permanent container','It spins up a fresh Docker container when a session is requested, binds the WebDriver port, and terminates/destroys the container immediately upon `driver.quit()`','It requires manual startup of Docker containers before every test run','It keeps 100 containers running idle at all times'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenoid dynamically provisions a container from the requested browser image when `new RemoteWebDriver` connects. Once the test calls `driver.quit()`, Selenoid automatically kills and cleans up the container, ensuring 100% test isolation.'},
{id:4250,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In Page Component design, how does creating a reusable `TableComponent` improve test maintainability?',options:['It replaces the database query layer','It encapsulates complex row/column traversal, header lookup, and pagination logic into helper methods (e.g., `getCellValue(row, col)`), preventing duplicated table XPath logic across tests','It renders HTML tables in the terminal','It converts HTML tables into Excel files automatically'],answer:1,explanation:'Tables often involve complex nested XPath and row/column index matching. Encapsulating table interactions in a dedicated `TableComponent` class allows any Page Object with a table to reuse methods like `getRowCount()`, `findRowByText()`, and `clickActionInRow()` cleanly.'},
{id:4251,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the best way to represent a reusable Modal Dialog in the Page Component pattern?',options:['By creating a separate `ModalDialogComponent` class with methods like `confirm()`, `cancel()`, `getModalTitle()`, and waiting for visibility of modal container element','By copy-pasting the modal locators into every Page Object that triggers the modal','By disabling modal dialogs in the application build settings','By handling the modal as an OS window alert'],answer:0,explanation:'A Modal Dialog is a self-contained UI component. Creating a dedicated `ModalDialogComponent` class with its own container locator, action methods (`clickConfirm()`, `clickDismiss()`), and wait conditions keeps page objects clean and modular.'},
{id:4252,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a Fluent Page Object design, why is returning `void` from page action methods generally avoided?',options:['Because Java methods cannot return void in modern frameworks','Because returning `void` prevents chaining consecutive actions and forces tests to explicitly declare and instantiate subsequent Page Objects repeatedly','Because void methods cause NullPointerException in TestNG','Because void methods disable assertion libraries'],answer:1,explanation:'Returning `void` breaks fluent method chaining. Returning the resulting Page Object (or `this`) allows test methods to flow naturally in clean descriptive chains like `homePage.search("Laptop").filterByPrice("500-1000").selectFirstProduct().addToCart();` without redundant intermediate reference declarations.'},
{id:4253,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can you use CDP Network Interception in Selenium 4 to simulate HTTP 500 server errors for specific API endpoints?',options:['By modifying the DNS settings of the test runner','By calling `DevTools.send(Fetch.enable(...))` and responding to matching requests with `Fetch.fulfillRequest` setting response code to 500','By disconnecting the network cable during the test','By passing `--server-error` into ChromeOptions'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenium 4 enables CDP Network/Fetch interception. Using `devTools.send(Fetch.enable(...))` and registering a listener on `Fetch.requestPaused`, tests can inspect request URLs and respond with `Fetch.fulfillRequest` providing `responseCode = 500` to test frontend error handling.'},
{id:4254,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you monitor HTTP response status codes (e.g., verifying no 404 or 500 errors occurred) during a Selenium 4 test run?',options:['By calling driver.getHttpStatusCode()','By attaching a listener to CDP `Network.responseReceived()` and storing response status codes for subsequent assertions','By parsing HTML body text for error numbers','By checking driver.manage().cookies()'],answer:1,explanation:'While standard WebDriver cannot read HTTP status codes, Selenium 4 CDP integration allows listening to `Network.responseReceived()` events to inspect HTTP headers, status codes (e.g. 200, 404, 500), and MIME types for all loaded resources.'},
{id:4255,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you wait for all active jQuery/AJAX requests to complete in a web page using Selenium `JavascriptExecutor`?',options:['driver.manage().timeouts().ajaxWait()','wait.until(driver -> (Boolean) ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript("return jQuery.active == 0"));','wait.until(ExpectedConditions.ajaxComplete())','Thread.sleep(3000)'],answer:1,explanation:'For jQuery-based applications, `jQuery.active` returns the number of currently pending AJAX requests. Waiting until `jQuery.active == 0` via `JavascriptExecutor` ensures all background asynchronous requests have finished before interacting with elements.'},
{id:4256,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you wait for Angular (v2+) HTTP requests and microtasks to settle using JavaScript in Selenium?',options:['wait.until(driver -> (Boolean) ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript("return window.getAllAngularTestabilities().findIndex(x => !x.isStable()) === -1"));','wait.until(ExpectedConditions.angularReady())','driver.manage().timeouts().angularWait()','By executing driver.navigate().refresh()'],answer:0,explanation:'Angular uses Zone.js testabilities under the hood. Executing `window.getAllAngularTestabilities().findIndex(x => !x.isStable()) === -1` via JavascriptExecutor checks whether all Angular testabilities on the page are completely stable and have no pending asynchronous microtasks or unresolved HTTP operations.'},
{id:4257,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'Why is locating elements by dynamic CSS class names like `.btn-primary__1x9Za` discouraged in Selenium?',options:['Because modern browsers do not support classes with underscores','Because hash-based class names generated by CSS Modules/styled-components change across builds, causing locator failures on future deployments','Because CSS selectors cannot match class names with numbers','Because Selenium cannot find elements with more than one class name'],answer:1,explanation:'Modern frontend build tools (Webpack, Vite, styled-components) generate randomized, hashed CSS class names that change whenever code is rebuilt. Locators relying on generated hashes break on subsequent builds, making them extremely fragile.'},
{id:4258,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does `By.cssSelector("button:not([disabled])")` help write robust Selenium tests?',options:['It finds all buttons and immediately disables them','It selects only button elements that are currently active and not disabled, preventing element click intercepted errors on disabled buttons','It forces the browser to bypass JavaScript validation','It selects buttons that do not have text'],answer:1,explanation:'The CSS `:not([disabled])` pseudo-class allows selecting only interactive, enabled buttons. This ensures the explicit wait condition waits until a disabled submit button becomes fully enabled before attempting to click it during test execution.'},
{id:4259,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference in CSS selector syntax between `div > span` and `div span` in Selenium?',options:['`div > span` matches only direct child `span` elements; `div span` matches any descendant `span` element at any depth inside the `div`','`div > span` matches spans with greater font size; `div span` matches all spans','Both selectors have identical performance and matching semantics','`div > span` is invalid CSS selector syntax'],answer:0,explanation:'In CSS selector syntax, `>` is the child combinator, matching only direct immediate children of the parent element. A space is the descendant combinator, matching elements nested at any depth within the ancestor.'},
{id:4260,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the CSS selector `div + p` select in Selenium WebDriver?',options:['All `div` and `p` elements on the page','The very first `p` element that immediately follows a `div` element as an adjacent sibling','All `p` elements inside a `div`','Any `div` that contains a `p` element'],answer:1,explanation:'The `+` adjacent sibling combinator matches the element that immediately follows the specified element on the same hierarchy level. `div + p` selects a `<p>` element placed directly after a `<div>`.'},
{id:4261,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the CSS selector `ul ~ p` select in Selenium?',options:['All `p` elements preceded by a `ul` element sharing the same parent (general sibling combinator)','All `p` elements nested inside a `ul` list','The parent container of both `ul` and `p`','Only the last `p` element on the page'],answer:0,explanation:'The `~` general sibling combinator matches all elements that follow the specified element and share the same parent node, even if they are separated by other intervening sibling elements on the page.'},
{id:4262,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you handle a scenario where clicking a link opens a new tab, but `driver.getWindowHandles()` does not immediately return 2 handles?',options:['Immediately fail the test','Use `WebDriverWait` with a custom condition or `ExpectedConditions.numberOfWindowsToBe(2)` before querying window handles','Call driver.quit() and restart the browser','Execute a JavaScript alert to count windows'],answer:1,explanation:'Opening a new window or tab is an asynchronous browser operation. Automated tests should synchronize using `new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(5)).until(ExpectedConditions.numberOfWindowsToBe(2))` before switching to ensure the new window handle is properly registered by WebDriver.'},
{id:4263,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In Selenide, what is the equivalent of Selenium\'s `driver.findElements(By.cssSelector("li"))`?',options:['Selenide.findAll("li") or $$("li")','Selenide.list("li")','driver.all("li")','ElementList("li")'],answer:0,explanation:'In Selenide, the `$$("li")` (or `Selenide.findAll("li")`) method returns an `ElementsCollection`. This provides rich fluent collection assertions such as `shouldHave(size(5))` and `shouldHave(texts(...))` with built-in dynamic retry, smart waiting, and polling mechanisms.'},
{id:4264,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In Selenide, what does `Selenide.open("https://example.com")` do automatically behind the scenes?',options:['It downloads an HTML file to the disk without launching a browser','It automatically initializes the configured browser instance (if not already open), sets timeouts, and navigates to the target URL','It connects to a Selenium Grid hub on port 8080','It compiles the test source code into native binary'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenide manages the browser lifecycle automatically. Calling `Selenide.open(url)` launches the browser driver instance on demand if one is not already running, configures default timeouts and capabilities, and navigates to the URL.'},
{id:4265,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you download a file and verify its contents easily in Selenide?',options:['By manually inspecting network packets via Wireshark','By calling `File file = $("#download-link").download();` and asserting on the returned `File` object','By using Java AWT Robot to navigate the OS save dialog','File downloads cannot be tested in Selenide'],answer:1,explanation:'Selenide provides a built-in `download()` method on elements: `File report = $("#download-btn").download();`. Selenide manages temporary download directory creation or internal proxy interception and returns the downloaded `File` object directly for immediate content assertion.'},
{id:4266,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you test a Chrome extension\'s background page or popup UI using Selenium WebDriver?',options:['Navigate directly to `chrome-extension://<EXTENSION_ID>/popup.html` or background page URL using `driver.get()`','Chrome extensions have no web URLs and cannot be navigated to','By taking a screenshot of the browser toolbar','By injecting C++ native hooks into Chrome'],answer:0,explanation:'Every loaded Chrome extension has a unique ID and serves its HTML pages (like `popup.html`, `options.html`) over `chrome-extension://<extension-id>/<page.html>`. WebDriver can navigate directly to these URLs to interact with extension UI elements.'},
{id:4267,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When using the AShot library for visual comparison testing, how do you compare two screenshot images for visual differences?',options:['ImageDiff diff = new ImageDiffer().makeDiff(expectedImage, actualImage); boolean hasDiff = diff.hasDiff();','expectedImage.equals(actualImage)','By comparing file size in bytes using File.length()','By hashing images with MD5 algorithm'],answer:0,explanation:'AShot provides the `ImageDiffer` utility. Calling `new ImageDiffer().makeDiff(expectedScreenshot, actualScreenshot)` creates an `ImageDiff` object, allowing automated tests to verify `!diff.hasDiff()` and generate diagnostic comparison images highlighting discrepancy pixels with customizable marker colors.'},
{id:4268,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you ignore dynamic, flashing, or changing page regions (like animated ads or timestamps) during visual testing with AShot?',options:['By disabling JavaScript globally in the browser','By adding `.addIgnoredElement(By.id("timestamp"))` or `.addIgnoredArea(coords)` to the `AShot` configuration','By running tests in monochrome mode','By setting monitor refresh rate to 0Hz'],answer:1,explanation:'AShot allows ignoring dynamic page elements during image comparison using `.addIgnoredElement(By.id("dynamic-widget"))` or `.ignoredAreas(...)`. The visual comparison engine ignores marked coordinates when detecting visual regressions between baseline and test run images.'},
{id:4269,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'How does AssertJ provide soft assertions for validating multiple Selenium elements without stopping on the first failure?',options:['Using `SoftAssertions softly = new SoftAssertions(); softly.assertThat(...); softly.assertAll();`','Using `Assert.failSoft()`','Using `try-catch` blocks around every assertion','AssertJ does not support soft assertions'],answer:0,explanation:'AssertJ supports soft assertions via `SoftAssertions` (or `AutoCloseableSoftAssertions`). Calling `softly.assertThat(...)` records all assertion failures without immediately throwing an AssertionError until `softly.assertAll()` is executed at the end of the test method.'},
{id:4270,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can you implement a custom `ExpectedCondition` that waits for an element\'s CSS attribute (like `background-color`) to transition to a specific value?',options:['Using driver.manage().timeouts().cssWait()','By returning an `ExpectedCondition<Boolean>` whose `apply(WebDriver driver)` checks `element.getCssValue(property).equals(targetValue)` and returns true once matched','By taking a screenshot and analyzing RGB pixel values','By refreshing the page repeatedly until CSS changes'],answer:1,explanation:'You implement an `ExpectedCondition<Boolean>` where the `apply` method queries `element.getCssValue("background-color")`. If the value matches the expected color (e.g. "rgba(0, 128, 0, 1)"), it returns `true`; otherwise it returns `false`, prompting FluentWait to continue polling.'},
{id:4271,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can you implement a custom `ExpectedCondition` that waits until a file is completely downloaded to a local directory in Selenium?',options:['Thread.sleep(10000)','By implementing `ExpectedCondition<Boolean>` that checks if the target file exists in the download directory and does not have a temporary `.crdownload` or `.part` extension','By querying the browser history via CDP','By verifying the network bandwidth utilization is 0'],answer:1,explanation:'Browsers append temporary extensions (like `.crdownload` in Chrome or `.part` in Firefox) while downloading. A custom `ExpectedCondition` checks that the destination file exists and that no temporary download files remain in the download directory.'},
{id:4272,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In TestNG, how can an `IAnnotationTransformer` be used to automatically apply a retry analyzer to all `@Test` annotations across an entire test suite?',options:['By adding @Retry to every Java class','By implementing `IAnnotationTransformer`, overriding `transform(ITestAnnotation annotation, ...)`, and calling `annotation.setRetryAnalyzer(Retry.class)`','By writing an XML parser for testng.xml','By passing -Dretry=all to Maven Surefire'],answer:1,explanation:'`IAnnotationTransformer` allows modifying TestNG annotations dynamically at runtime. Overriding `transform(...)` to call `annotation.setRetryAnalyzer(MyRetry.class)` automatically attaches retry logic to every `@Test` method across the entire test suite without requiring manual per-method configuration.'},
{id:4273,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a drawback of using automated test retries (e.g., `IRetryAnalyzer`) as a permanent solution for flaky tests?',options:['It reduces test execution time','It masks real application bugs, hides underlying synchronization flaws, and significantly increases overall CI build execution time','It causes Maven compiler errors','It deletes failed test execution reports'],answer:1,explanation:'While retries can prevent intermittent CI build failures, relying on them permanently hides genuine race conditions and real application flakiness, while increasing execution time as failing tests run multiple times before passing or failing.'},
{id:4274,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does running Selenium tests in Headless mode affect execution performance and resource usage?',options:['Headless mode consumes twice as much CPU and RAM as GUI mode','Headless mode reduces memory overhead and CPU rendering costs since the OS window manager does not render pixels to a display, resulting in faster execution on CI servers','Headless mode disables all JavaScript execution in the browser','Headless mode is only compatible with Firefox'],answer:1,explanation:'Headless browsers skip OS window rendering, GPU compositing, and screen buffer painting. This drastically reduces CPU and memory footprint, speeding up page processing and making it ideal for high-density CI/CD container execution.'},
{id:4275,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Why should you avoid creating a new `WebDriver` instance inside every single `@Test` method if tests can run with a shared session safely?',options:['Because ChromeDriver cannot be initialized more than once per day','Because spinning up and shutting down a browser process takes several seconds of CPU and I/O overhead, multiplying total test suite runtime across hundreds of tests','Because WebDriver requires a paid license for each instance','Because browsers run out of port numbers after 10 launches'],answer:1,explanation:'Launching a browser process and establishing a WebDriver session introduces significant overhead (typically 2–5 seconds per browser start). When appropriate, reusing sessions across independent read-only tests significantly reduces suite runtime.'},
{id:4276,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'beginner',question:'In Gauge framework, how are test specifications organized and written?',options:['In JSON files with key-value pairs','In `.spec` or `.md` files using plain Markdown headers (`# Spec Title`), bullet points (`* Step description`), and tables','In XML documents adhering to XSD schema','In binary compiled bytecode files'],answer:1,explanation:'Gauge specifications are written in standard Markdown files. `#` denotes specification and scenario headers, while `*` denotes executable steps that map directly to underlying Java/Python/C# step implementations in the test framework.'},
{id:4277,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In Robot Framework with SeleniumLibrary, how do you handle explicit waits for element visibility?',options:['Thread.sleep(5)','Wait Until Element Is Visible    locator    timeout=10s','Sleep Until Visible    locator','driver.wait(locator)'],answer:1,explanation:'Robot Framework\'s SeleniumLibrary provides built-in dynamic wait keywords like `Wait Until Element Is Visible    xpath=//button    10s` and `Wait Until Page Contains Element`, avoiding hardcoded `Sleep` keywords in keyword-driven test cases.'},
{id:4278,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How can you configure Maven Surefire to rerun failing Selenium tests automatically without custom Java code?',options:['By adding `<rerunFailingTestsCount>2</rerunFailingTestsCount>` in the `maven-surefire-plugin` configuration in pom.xml','By running `mvn test --retry 2`','By setting `surefire.retry=true` in settings.xml','By annotating the pom.xml with @Retry'],answer:0,explanation:'Maven Surefire plugin provides built-in test retry support via the `<rerunFailingTestsCount>N</rerunFailingTestsCount>` configuration tag (supported for JUnit and TestNG), automatically rerunning failing tests up to N times during the build process.'},
{id:4279,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In Maven Failsafe plugin, what goal binds to the `verify` phase to ensure failed integration tests fail the build after cleanup?',options:['failsafe:run','failsafe:verify','failsafe:teardown','failsafe:check'],answer:1,explanation:'The `failsafe:integration-test` goal executes the tests during the integration-test phase without failing the build immediately. The `failsafe:verify` goal bound to the `verify` phase then inspects test results and fails the build if any test failed.'},
{id:4280,topic:'selenium',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What was the primary architectural motivation for replacing the JSON Wire Protocol with the W3C WebDriver Standard in Selenium 4?',options:['To make Selenium proprietary software','To standardize browser automation directly within the W3C web standards body, eliminating protocol translation encoding/decoding overhead between browser drivers and client libraries','To remove support for Chrome and Firefox','To prevent browser drivers from using HTTP ports'],answer:1,explanation:'The W3C WebDriver Standard made browser automation an official web standard natively implemented by browser vendors. This eliminated the legacy JSON Wire Protocol translation layer, resulting in greater stability, standardized capabilities, and consistent cross-browser behavior.'}
